0
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,660


1
00:00:03,660 --> 00:00:07,090
So in this example, we want to hit

2
00:00:07,090 --> 00:00:10,360
an apple that's hanging from a tree with a projectile.

3
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:12,200
And the main point of the problem

4
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:14,440
is to figure out at what angle to the ground

5
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:17,340
should we aim our projectile when we fire it off

6
00:00:17,340 --> 00:00:18,570
in order to hit the apple.

7
00:00:18,570 --> 00:00:20,150
And in this example, we're assuming

8
00:00:20,150 --> 00:00:22,860
that the apple drops from the tree at the same instant

9
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:24,590
that we fire the projectile.

10
00:00:24,590 --> 00:00:27,590
So in this drawing, let's just put some dimensions in here.

11
00:00:27,590 --> 00:00:29,470
We'll assume that the apple starts out

12
00:00:29,470 --> 00:00:34,520
at a height h above the ground, that the horizontal distance

13
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:39,360
from where the projectile starts to the apple

14
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:46,708
is a distance d, that the projectile starts at a distance

15
00:00:46,708 --> 00:00:53,990
s above the ground, and that we fire the projectile off

16
00:00:53,990 --> 00:00:59,440
with an initial velocity v0 at an angle theta

17
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:04,280
0 with respect to the horizontal.

18
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:08,780
And let's define our origin to be

19
00:01:08,780 --> 00:01:12,870
right here on the ground directly

20
00:01:12,870 --> 00:01:17,550
below where the projectile begins.

21
00:01:17,550 --> 00:01:19,310
We need to define our coordinate system.

22
00:01:19,310 --> 00:01:24,440
So the i hat direction will be horizontally to the right,

23
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:27,250
and the j hat direction will be vertically upwards

24
00:01:27,250 --> 00:01:29,320
with an origin at this point.

25
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:33,210
So the question that we have is, what should our angle theta 0

26
00:01:33,210 --> 00:01:37,100
be in order to hit the apple, which

27
00:01:37,100 --> 00:01:39,250
starts falling from the tree at the same instant

28
00:01:39,250 --> 00:01:40,990
that we fire our projectile, and we

29
00:01:40,990 --> 00:01:43,790
want to hit the apple before it hits the ground?

30
00:01:43,790 --> 00:01:46,900
OK, so to work this out, we need to consider

31
00:01:46,900 --> 00:01:50,170
the kinematics of two separate objects, our projectile

32
00:01:50,170 --> 00:01:51,520
and the apple.

33
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:55,070
So let's begin with the apple.

34
00:01:55,070 --> 00:02:03,101
So for our apple, the apple drops vertically straight

35
00:02:03,101 --> 00:02:03,600
downward.

36
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:05,660
So its horizontal position throughout its motion

37
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:08,750
is just unchanged.

38
00:02:08,750 --> 00:02:12,570
So the x-coordinate of the apple as a function of time

39
00:02:12,570 --> 00:02:17,310
is just d, it's just a constant.

40
00:02:17,310 --> 00:02:24,960
Its y-coordinate, it starts out at a height h,

41
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:28,950
and then it drops due to the acceleration of gravity.

42
00:02:28,950 --> 00:02:33,240
And so that's given by minus 1/2 gt squared.

43
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:35,780
It's a minus because it's falling in the minus j hat

44
00:02:35,780 --> 00:02:36,875
direction.

45
00:02:36,875 --> 00:02:38,375
Now, for our projectile-- let's call

46
00:02:38,375 --> 00:02:49,540
it a bullet-- for our bullet, the x-coordinate of the bullet,

47
00:02:49,540 --> 00:02:53,210
it starts out at the origin, at x equals 0.

48
00:02:53,210 --> 00:02:56,653
And its initial motion, it has an initial velocity v0

49
00:02:56,653 --> 00:02:58,120
in this direction.

50
00:02:58,120 --> 00:03:00,680
The horizontal component of that is v0

51
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:02,330
times the cosine of theta 0l.

52
00:03:02,330 --> 00:03:05,010
And so its displacement at time t

53
00:03:05,010 --> 00:03:09,910
is v0 t times the cosine of theta 0.

54
00:03:09,910 --> 00:03:11,540
And there's no horizontal acceleration,

55
00:03:11,540 --> 00:03:14,330
so that completes our x-coordinate

56
00:03:14,330 --> 00:03:15,810
as a function of time.

57
00:03:15,810 --> 00:03:20,610
For our y-coordinate, we start out

58
00:03:20,610 --> 00:03:23,400
at a height s above the ground.

59
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:25,180
There is the change in the y-coordinate

60
00:03:25,180 --> 00:03:28,760
due to the initial velocity, which has component v0

61
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:29,880
sine theta 0.

62
00:03:29,880 --> 00:03:38,010
So that's plus v0 t sine theta 0.

63
00:03:38,010 --> 00:03:40,260
And there's also a vertical acceleration

64
00:03:40,260 --> 00:03:48,330
due to gravity, which gives us minus 1/2 gt squared.

65
00:03:48,330 --> 00:03:51,460
So that's the kinematics of the apple and the bullet.

66
00:03:51,460 --> 00:03:58,609
Now, for there to be a hit at time t equals capital T,

67
00:03:58,609 --> 00:04:00,400
the coordinates of the apple and the bullet

68
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:03,880
have to be the same at that collision time.

69
00:04:03,880 --> 00:04:13,270
So for a hit at t equals big T, we

70
00:04:13,270 --> 00:04:17,350
require that the x-coordinate of the bullet at time big T

71
00:04:17,350 --> 00:04:20,610
is the same as the x-coordinate of the apple,

72
00:04:20,610 --> 00:04:23,770
and likewise that the y-coordinate of the bullet

73
00:04:23,770 --> 00:04:27,310
is equal to the y-coordinate of the apple.

74
00:04:27,310 --> 00:04:29,860
OK, so let's look at each of these in turn.

75
00:04:29,860 --> 00:04:36,550
For our x-coordinate, the x-coordinate of the bullet

76
00:04:36,550 --> 00:04:42,710
is v0 capital T times the cosine of theta 0,

77
00:04:42,710 --> 00:04:47,140
and that has to be equal to the x-coordinate

78
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:48,980
of the apple, which is just d.

79
00:04:48,980 --> 00:04:52,900
So notice that I can solve this for the time of the collision,

80
00:04:52,900 --> 00:04:59,780
capital T. And that's just d minus v0 times

81
00:04:59,780 --> 00:05:03,660
the cosine of theta 0.

82
00:05:03,660 --> 00:05:09,590
Now, for our y-coordinate, the y-coordinate of the bullet

83
00:05:09,590 --> 00:05:21,100
is s plus v0 capital T times the sine of theta not minus 1/2 gt

84
00:05:21,100 --> 00:05:23,260
squared.

85
00:05:23,260 --> 00:05:25,740
And that's equal to the y-coordinate

86
00:05:25,740 --> 00:05:32,320
of the apple, which is h minus 1/2 gt squared.

87
00:05:32,320 --> 00:05:37,070


88
00:05:37,070 --> 00:05:37,760
All right.

89
00:05:37,760 --> 00:05:39,840
Now, I can rearrange that.

90
00:05:39,840 --> 00:05:43,077
Notice that I have a minus 1/2 gt squared on both sides,

91
00:05:43,077 --> 00:05:43,910
so those cancel out.

92
00:05:43,910 --> 00:05:48,660
I can rearrange that to write v0 times capital T

93
00:05:48,660 --> 00:05:56,220
times the sine of theta 0 is equal to h minus s.

94
00:05:56,220 --> 00:06:01,070
So that the sine of theta 0 is equal to h

95
00:06:01,070 --> 00:06:06,100
minus s over v0 capital T.

96
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:10,130
But remember, we solved for capital T here.

97
00:06:10,130 --> 00:06:11,350
So I can substitute that in.

98
00:06:11,350 --> 00:06:13,900


99
00:06:13,900 --> 00:06:24,530
So that gives me h minus s over v0 times 1 over t,

100
00:06:24,530 --> 00:06:34,170
which is v0 cosine theta 0 over d.

101
00:06:34,170 --> 00:06:42,500
And so I can rewrite that as h minus s over d

102
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:44,154
times the cosine of theta 0.

103
00:06:44,154 --> 00:06:47,510


104
00:06:47,510 --> 00:06:50,010
So rewriting that, I have on the left hand side

105
00:06:50,010 --> 00:06:55,050
sine of theta 0 over cosine of theta 0

106
00:06:55,050 --> 00:06:59,436
is equal to h minus s over d.

107
00:06:59,436 --> 00:07:01,760
And notice on the left hand side sine over cosine

108
00:07:01,760 --> 00:07:09,150
is just tangent, so this is just the tangent of theta 0.

109
00:07:09,150 --> 00:07:10,920
Now, think about what that means.

110
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:20,260
If I draw a right triangle where this is theta 0

111
00:07:20,260 --> 00:07:23,360
and this is h minus s and this is

112
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:26,380
d, and so this is the same geometry

113
00:07:26,380 --> 00:07:30,950
we have here if we drew the triangle like this.

114
00:07:30,950 --> 00:07:35,430
So theta 0 is just the angle to the location of the apple

115
00:07:35,430 --> 00:07:37,380
just before it drops.

116
00:07:37,380 --> 00:07:39,091
So what this calculation tells us

117
00:07:39,091 --> 00:07:41,590
is that the correct thing to do if we want to hit the apple,

118
00:07:41,590 --> 00:07:43,923
if we know the apple's going to drop at the same instant

119
00:07:43,923 --> 00:07:47,260
that we fire, then we should aim at the location

120
00:07:47,260 --> 00:07:49,950
that the apple is at that instant.

121
00:07:49,950 --> 00:07:53,250
We shouldn't try and lead the apple and fire below it

122
00:07:53,250 --> 00:07:54,210
or fire above it.

123
00:07:54,210 --> 00:07:56,050
We should aim directly at the apple.

124
00:07:56,050 --> 00:07:58,120
And what the kinematics tells us is

125
00:07:58,120 --> 00:07:59,650
that both the bullet and the apple

126
00:07:59,650 --> 00:08:01,650
fall vertically down at the same rate.

127
00:08:01,650 --> 00:08:04,710
And so that will give us a collision.

128
00:08:04,710 --> 00:08:08,590
Now, it's worth thinking about two different cases,

129
00:08:08,590 --> 00:08:10,870
which I'm not going to solve for you here.

130
00:08:10,870 --> 00:08:14,770
The first is, what if the apple didn't drop?

131
00:08:14,770 --> 00:08:19,010
Suppose the apple just stayed in the tree, and I fired?

132
00:08:19,010 --> 00:08:22,584
If I aimed directly at the apple and the apple didn't drop,

133
00:08:22,584 --> 00:08:24,750
then I would miss the apple because the bullet would

134
00:08:24,750 --> 00:08:29,210
drop as it went across this distance d.

135
00:08:29,210 --> 00:08:32,080
Now, of course, if the apple were big enough,

136
00:08:32,080 --> 00:08:34,830
or if the bullet were flying fast enough, if v0

137
00:08:34,830 --> 00:08:38,580
was fast enough, then it might be

138
00:08:38,580 --> 00:08:40,225
that the amount that the bullet dropped

139
00:08:40,225 --> 00:08:42,350
wouldn't be bigger than the thickness of the apple.

140
00:08:42,350 --> 00:08:44,059
So I might still graze the apple.

141
00:08:44,059 --> 00:08:46,020
But I wouldn't hit the apple dead center.

142
00:08:46,020 --> 00:08:49,072
So if I knew that the apple wasn't going to drop,

143
00:08:49,072 --> 00:08:50,530
and I wanted to hit it dead center,

144
00:08:50,530 --> 00:08:53,370
I would have to choose some different angle, theta 0l.

145
00:08:53,370 --> 00:08:54,820
And we can sort of see intuitively

146
00:08:54,820 --> 00:08:58,290
that that angle would have to be a little bit steeper so

147
00:08:58,290 --> 00:09:00,859
that the bullet would drop and then hit the apple.

148
00:09:00,859 --> 00:09:02,400
And so the way we would solve that is

149
00:09:02,400 --> 00:09:04,950
in our original kinematics equations,

150
00:09:04,950 --> 00:09:08,460
for the motion of the apple, x would still

151
00:09:08,460 --> 00:09:11,750
be a constant d, but if the apple wasn't dropping, then

152
00:09:11,750 --> 00:09:13,590
y of the apple would also be a constant.

153
00:09:13,590 --> 00:09:15,070
We wouldn't have the second term.

154
00:09:15,070 --> 00:09:18,070
We would just have y equals h.

155
00:09:18,070 --> 00:09:21,190
And then I would have to solve for a collision.

156
00:09:21,190 --> 00:09:22,780
The other case to think about that I'd

157
00:09:22,780 --> 00:09:25,530
like you to think about is, what if the apple

158
00:09:25,530 --> 00:09:29,800
begins dropping for a short interval before we fired?

159
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:32,310
So that at the time that we fire our projectile,

160
00:09:32,310 --> 00:09:34,840
the apple is already dropping.

161
00:09:34,840 --> 00:09:37,080
Well, that's equivalent to saying that at time equals

162
00:09:37,080 --> 00:09:40,910
0 when I fire, that the apple has some initial velocity,

163
00:09:40,910 --> 00:09:43,730
whatever velocity it's picked up by dropping

164
00:09:43,730 --> 00:09:45,680
in whatever interval it was dropping before.

165
00:09:45,680 --> 00:09:48,990
So now my kinematics for my apple

166
00:09:48,990 --> 00:09:50,620
in the vertical direction, I would

167
00:09:50,620 --> 00:09:53,870
have an additional term here, a minus v0 t,

168
00:09:53,870 --> 00:09:55,462
where v0 is that initial velocity.

169
00:09:55,462 --> 00:09:57,970


170
00:09:57,970 --> 00:09:59,157
And that's a different v0.

171
00:09:59,157 --> 00:10:00,740
I should have used a different symbol.

172
00:10:00,740 --> 00:10:02,656
It's a different v0 than the v0 of the bullet.

173
00:10:02,656 --> 00:10:05,550
But there would be an initial velocity, falling velocity,

174
00:10:05,550 --> 00:10:07,680
of the apple that I'd have to consider.

175
00:10:07,680 --> 00:10:10,720
So the particular details of what's happening with the apple

176
00:10:10,720 --> 00:10:11,850
change the kinematics.

177
00:10:11,850 --> 00:10:13,225
And it's worth thinking about how

178
00:10:13,225 --> 00:10:15,100
that would change your answer.

